# How the three locations relate

```text
Cursor
   ↓ Save
Desktop project files
   ↓ git add
Staging area
   ↓ git commit
Local Git history
   ↓ git push
GitHub repository
```

Cursor is not a separate copy of your project. Cursor is simply editing the files stored in:

```text
C:\Users\rohit\Desktop\AI_Desktop_Robotic_Arm
```

Git is built into that project through a hidden folder called:

```text
.git
```

GitHub is the remote online repository.

---

# What happens when you only edit and save?

Suppose you update `Roadmap.md` and stop after `Ctrl + S`.

The desktop file is updated, but GitHub remains unchanged.

```text
Cursor/Desktop: New version
GitHub: Old version
```

Your changes are not lost, but they exist only on your computer.

---

# What happens after `git add .` only?

The changes are prepared for a commit, but no snapshot has been created yet.

```text
Desktop: Updated
Staging area: Updated
Local history: Not updated
GitHub: Not updated
```

---

# What happens after `git commit` but before `git push`?

The changes are safely recorded in your computer's local Git history.

```text
Desktop: Updated
Local Git: Updated
GitHub: Old version
```

If your computer fails before pushing, the commit may still be lost with the computer because it has not been uploaded.

---

# What happens after `git push`?

Everything is synchronized:

```text
Desktop project = Local Git commit = GitHub repository
```

More precisely, GitHub matches your latest **committed** version. Any edits made after that commit will still remain only on your computer until you repeat the cycle.

---

## Important: empty folders do not appear on GitHub

Git tracks files, not empty folders.

For example, if these folders contain nothing:

```text
AI/
CAD/
Firmware/
Media/
Simulation/
Vision/
```

they might not appear on GitHub.

To keep an empty folder visible, create a file inside it named:

```text
.gitkeep
```

Example:

```text
Media/.gitkeep
```

Then commit and push it:

```powershell
git add .
git commit -m "Add project directory structure"
git push
```

---

## Best habit for this project

Do not commit after every sentence or every line of code. Commit after a meaningful unit of work.

Good examples:

```text
Add initial ROS 2 package
Implement forward kinematics
Update project roadmap
Add gripper CAD files
Fix joint angle calculation
Add object detection prototype
```

Avoid unclear messages such as:

```text
changes
update
work
new stuff
final
```

Your everyday formula is:

```powershell
git status
git add .
git commit -m "What you completed"
git push
```

That is how you update your desktop project, local Git history, and GitHub repository.
